# tests/known_fail/dd14_bc_open.rxt -- [DD-14] wave B+C's TWO OPEN CELLS
#
# THIS DIRECTORY IS EXCLUDED FROM `make test` AND RUN BY THE RATCHET, which
# fails if a cell here starts PASSING. That is the point: both cells below
# record what libpcre2 10.46 says, pcrec currently says something else, and
# NOBODY HAS RULED which answer pcrec should ship. If a later wave changes
# either, this file fires and the ruling gets made deliberately.
#
# It is `u9_atomic.rxt`'s shape, and that file's own header states the
# principle: "the cells stay, they stay loud, and if pcrec is ever changed to
# reproduce it this file FIRES". These two are the opposite polarity from
# U9's -- there pcrec disagrees with libpcre2 and pcrec is believed right;
# here pcrec OVER-REJECTS what libpcre2 accepts and matches, and the refusal
# is a consequence of WHEN a pass runs rather than of any ruling, which is why
# it needs a charter rather than a patch.
#
# Every expectation below is copied from `tests/recursion/`'s generated
# corpus, i.e. from libpcre2 10.46 through
# docs/design/subroutines_measurements/probes/sr_oracle.py. The live corpus
# carries a comment stanza at each cell's former position pointing here;
# `tests/recursion/gen_corpus.py`'s `parked=` argument is what writes it, so
# the two cannot drift.
#
# A THIRD CELL WAS HERE AND WAS WRONG TO BE (removed 2026-08-24, manager
# review). `^(a?(?1)b)$` -- a left recursion whose language is empty -- was
# parked as an unruled disagreement because pcrec answers NOMATCH where the
# corpus expected `gu frames`. **The corpus was wrong, not pcrec**: design
# SS12 P-12 RULES that minw = infinity is a legal compile the MRL prune reads
# as "no position can match", so the constant-time nomatch IS the ruled
# answer, and a give-up is pcrec's own artifact behaviour, never an oracle
# fact to be pinned from libpcre2's rc -52. The cell is now live in
# `tests/recursion/leftrec.rxt` as a RULED nomatch carrying its citation.
# The lesson generalises: **this file is for pcrec disagreeing with a RULING,
# not for pcrec disagreeing with an EXPECTATION nobody checked against one.**

# ====================================================================
# CELLS 1 and 2 -- A CALL INSIDE A LOOKBEHIND, over-rejected
# ====================================================================
#
# pcrec REFUSES both ("variable-length lookbehind is not implemented: every
# alternative of a lookbehind must have a fixed length (this one is
# unbounded)") where 10.46 ACCEPTS and matches. A tier-2 OVER-REJECTION, never
# a miscompile.
#
# THE CAUSE IS TIMING, NOT THE WIDTH ANALYSIS. `pcrec_maxw`'s `A_CALL` arm
# answers `PCREC_W_UNBOUNDED`, which design §3.4(d) makes EXACT for a
# recursive callee (10.46 refuses that itself, err 125) and a sound
# over-estimate for every other. Tightening it for an ACYCLIC callee needs the
# call graph — and `la_widths` (src/parse/mod_lookaround.c) runs INSIDE THE
# PARSE HOOK, where it must, because that is the only place with a pattern
# OFFSET to refuse at, while the graph does not exist until every call is
# resolved at end of parse and every rewriting pass has run. **So no `A_CALL`
# arm of `pcrec_maxw` can make these compile**: the width is decided before
# the callee is known. Design §3.4(d) says "the width analysis descends into
# `A_CALL` by descending into the callee" and does not address when it runs.
#
# THE FIX, for whoever rules on it: a DEFERRED WIDTH RE-CHECK — the parse hook
# records the lookbehind (and its offset) instead of refusing when its body
# carries a call, and a pass after `pcrec_callgraph_build` recomputes the
# widths and refuses then. It is a change to a landed module's core plus a new
# `u.look` field for the diagnostic's offset, which wave B+C did not take on.

# libpcre2 10.46: "ab" -> (0,2). The callee is fixed width 2.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>ab)){0}ab(?<=(?&g))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "ab" 0 2

# libpcre2 10.46: "ab" -> (0,2). Widths 1 and 2 -- the fixed-PER-BRANCH form
# pcrec's own lookbehind subset ships (lookaround_design.md §2.5), so this one
# is inside the shipped subset and still refused.
pattern ^(?:(?<g>a|ab)){0}ab(?<=(?&g))$
features recursion,lookaround,atomic-groups,named-groups
m "ab" 0 2
